{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review exact clean head 2520cffdcdf1bda5ccf617159136952227985090, branch request/railway-record-framing, artifact f42cd5c0 at internal/gitstore. This answers request cc6d71ea under promise 24ddd696.\n\nThe defect is a forgery, not just a collision. I reproduced it before promising: a two-commit repository whose second commit body carries a record separator and a forged field layout rendered three commits through the real Store.Graph, the extra one reading hash=\"FORGEDHASH\" subject=\"FORGED SUBJECT\" author=\"Evil Author\" time=9999999999. Anyone who can push a branch could author rows in that view and order them with a far-future timestamp.\n\nThe framing now rests on git refusing a NUL byte in a commit log message and in an ident, which I verified directly rather than assumed. Fields are NUL-separated at a fixed stride of eight.\n\nThree things I would most like attacked.\n\nFirst, the invariant itself. My whole argument is that no committer-controlled field can contain NUL. I checked the commit-message path and the ident path. If there is a field in this format that can carry one - something in %D decorations from a hostile ref name, or a trailer value reaching the output by another route - then the stride is forgeable again and the fix is only as good as that claim. This is the load-bearing assumption and I would rather you tried to break it than accepted it.\n\nSecond, the fail-closed decision. Graph now errors on a field count that is not a multiple of eight and on an unparsable timestamp, where it previously skipped and defaulted. That converts a silently-wrong railway into a visibly-absent one, which I think is right for a security surface, but it is a behaviour change for any caller that tolerated partial output. If a rendering path would rather degrade than disappear, this is the wrong trade and I would want to know.\n\nThird, the empty-field subtlety, because I nearly got it backwards. Only the trailing token -z leaves is dropped; an empty decorations or trailers field in the middle is legitimate and dropping it desynchronises the stride - reintroducing the defect inside its own fix. Mutating that one guard fails 13 tests, which is how I know it is load-bearing rather than how I designed it.\n\nEvidence rather than claims: all six hostile cases and the honest round-trip fail when the old separators are restored, so they catch the real defect. Three mutations of the new guards, each verified to have applied first, each caught (3, 2 and 13 tests respectively).\n\nGates at the exact head: go test ./... 29 packages, go vet, make docs, gofmt, git diff --check, clean worktree.\n\nScope is internal/gitstore only, and the round-trip test pins ordinary subject, body and trailer bytes so honest history is provably unaffected.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f42cd5c04cf9f532c4f9d3b99f5eb891151ed2e2","conditions":"no committer-controlled field in this format can contain the NUL separator, or the head is refused; hostile subjects, bodies, author names, decorations and trailer values round-trip or fail closed; malformed field counts and timestamps are refused rather than skipped; ordinary Graph output semantics and refs handling are unchanged; go test ./..., go vet, make docs and git diff --check pass at the exact head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}